    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d435">

                <pb facs="1105" n="991" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg991"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    J.C.S. Files</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the United States Chiefs of
                        Staff</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d435-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        C.C.S. 315/1</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quebec</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-08-19">19 August 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">“<hi rendition="#small-caps">Habbakuks</hi>”</p>
                <p>The U.S. Chiefs of Staff have given careful consideration to the proposals
                    submitted by the British Chiefs of Staff in C.C.S. 315<anchor n="2" xml:id="d435-f2"/> and have reached the
                    following conclusions: <pb facs="1106" n="992" xml:id="pg992"/>
                    <list>
                        <label>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>.</label>
                        <item>By the expenditure of extraordinary effort and consequent stoppage of
                            other essential war projects, the construction of 
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habbakuk II</hi>
                             and an erecting plant therefor is feasible and might be
                            completed as early as the end of 1945.</item>
                        <label>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Construction of 
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habbakuk III</hi>
                             could possibly be accomplished by the end of 1945. Claims for
                            invulnerability of 
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habbakuk III</hi>
                             to hull damage may be somewhat justified, but they are
                            outweighed by the operating advantages inherent in conventional carrier
                            types by virtue of speed, maneuverability, and operating
                            refinements.</item>
                        <label>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>.</label>
                        <item>Due to the relatively small value of the <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                                Habbakuks</hi> in increasing
                            the effectiveness of aircraft operation, and in view of the existing
                            aircraft carrier program, the diversion of manpower and critical
                            materials involved in their construction is not warranted.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
